august 25th marks five years since  nearly 740 000 mostly muslim rohingya  fled to bangladesh from myanmar  a flight triggered by a military  crackdown and widespread atrocities  un investigators and the u.s have called  it a genocide  bashley spoke to rohingya women youth  leaders and religious representatives in  the cox bazaar refugee camp  we told her that what we were not able  to do in myanmar we were able to do here  but we want to return to our home  she assured us that the un is working  hard and that soon we'll be able to  start the repatriation process  this is the first time a un high  commissioner for human rights has  visited bangladesh and the cox's bazaar  rohingya refugee camp since the post was  established 29 years ago  bangladesh's foreign minister arju  bashley to engage with myanmar and  ensure the repatriation of forcibly  displaced rohingya  the u.n high commissioner has shown much  concern about the rohingya and the u.n  has appreciated all our help they also  want bangladesh to continue to help the  rohingya and say they will stand beside  us on this  one refugee presented a letter to the  unhigh commissioner on behalf of all of  them  i wrote we escaped to bangladesh due to  atrocity committed against us women were  raped and burned to death there are  still some rohingya whose confiscated  land is not returned despite promises  made  if the myanmar government accepts all of  our rights and demands only then we will  return  bashley told refugees those forcibly  displaced would be repatriated under u.n  supervision and voluntarily and  diplomatic efforts were underway to make  that happen  but many here wonder how long that will  take and whether myanmar's government  will ever accept their demands can be  chaudhary al-jazeera daca
 

